+# Decision: Self-Hosted WebSockets for Change Delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard maintains one authoritative board per event. Editors submit state changes over HTTPS, and up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across 40 weekend markets must see committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, including opening-time bursts of up to 20 edits per second. Browsers disconnected for up to 20 minutes must recover the latest board state.
+
+While HTTPS commands are uniform regardless of how updates reach browsers, the change-delivery transport shapes three downstream components together: **change-store** (how revisions are indexed, replayed, and published), **delivery** (how server-to-browser transport is hosted and scaled), and **browser-sync** (how clients subscribe, detect disconnection, and catch up). Because all three candidate transports can meet the 15-second freshness goal, the choice turns on latency, vendor coupling, operational burden, and how cleanly each transport aligns with authoritative event revisions and visible connectivity failures.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+All three options satisfy the 15-second freshness goal and the HLD tenet permitting both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, so no gate eliminates an option. Selection is driven by four weighted criteria:
+
+- **Vendor independence and cost predictability (major)** — Avoiding recurring per-connection or per-message realtime vendor bills and avoiding coupling core delivery semantics to a third-party provider's SDK and availability model.
+- **Update freshness and burst efficiency (moderate)** — Delivering committed changes quickly (subsecond when connected) during 20-edit/sec opening-time bursts without multiplying read queries across 800 browsers.
+- **Operational simplicity of connection management (moderate)** — Minimizing stateful server connection capacity, sticky/routed session lifecycle management, and custom reconnect orchestration.
+- **Alignment with authoritative revisions and visible failure (moderate)** — Turns on the HLD tenets *A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route* and *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*. Measures how directly the transport exposes server-assigned revision gaps and connection loss without an intermediate state layer obscuring them.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+#### Description
+
+Browsers issue a periodic HTTPS `GET` every five seconds carrying their last applied event revision. The **change-store** serves cursor-based revision queries (or full snapshots when a client is too far behind); **delivery** remains stateless request/response behind standard HTTP load balancing; and **browser-sync** runs a polling timer that applies returned deltas and surfaces connectivity failure when consecutive polls fail or time out.
+
+#### Analysis
+
+- **Vendor independence and cost predictability**: **strong** — Uses standard HTTPS infrastructure with no external realtime service fees or provider SDK coupling.
+- **Update freshness and burst efficiency**: **weak** — Introduces up to 5 seconds of polling lag under normal conditions and generates ~160 read requests per second across 800 idle or active browsers regardless of whether edits are occurring.
+- **Operational simplicity of connection management**: **strong** — Delivery stays stateless; no long-lived socket capacity planning, connection draining, or WebSocket session state is required.
+- **Alignment with authoritative revisions and visible failure**: **partial** — Revision cursors map directly to authoritative event revisions, though silent intervals between polls delay detection of a dropped network path until the next poll interval expires.
+
+#### Summary
+
+Simplest stateless server operation, traded for multi-second update lag and continuous repeated reads across 800 browsers.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+#### Description
+
+Browsers maintain a persistent WebSocket connection to the **delivery** service, which broadcasts committed event revisions as they arrive from the **change-store**'s reliable publication mechanism. When a socket drops or a browser reconnects within the 20-minute recovery window, **browser-sync** reconnects and requests missed revisions (or a snapshot) from **change-store** using its last applied revision.
+
+#### Analysis
+
+- **Vendor independence and cost predictability**: **strong** — Runs on self-hosted infrastructure with no recurring realtime vendor bill or third-party provider coupling.
+- **Update freshness and burst efficiency**: **strong** — Pushes committed revisions immediately (subsecond) and avoids repeated polling reads when boards are idle or during 20-edit/sec bursts.
+- **Operational simplicity of connection management**: **weak** — Requires owning WebSocket session lifecycle, 800-browser connection capacity, graceful draining, reliable store-to-delivery publication, and client reconnect/catch-up logic.
+- **Alignment with authoritative revisions and visible failure**: **strong** — Socket close and heartbeat timeouts surface connectivity loss immediately, and every pushed frame carries the server-assigned authoritative revision for gap detection and replay.
+
+#### Summary
+
+Fast updates and full vendor independence, traded for owning stateful socket capacity, reliable publication, and reconnect/catch-up machinery.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+#### Description
+
+The server publishes committed revisions to a managed realtime provider (e.g., managed pub/sub or WebSocket-as-a-service), and **browser-sync** subscribes using the provider's client SDK and credentials. **Delivery** wraps provider authentication and channel mapping, while **change-store** reconciles outbox publication with the provider so missed or out-of-order provider deliveries can be recovered.
+
+#### Analysis
+
+- **Vendor independence and cost predictability**: **weak** — Introduces recurring usage-based vendor fees and couples delivery and browser subscription semantics to a specific provider's SDK, limits, and outage behavior.
+- **Update freshness and burst efficiency**: **strong** — Delivers subsecond push updates without self-hosted polling load or direct socket fan-out on application servers.
+- **Operational simplicity of connection management**: **partial** — Offloads raw 800-browser socket termination, but still requires managing provider credentials, publication reconciliation in the change store, and provider-specific reconnect/resync behavior.
+- **Alignment with authoritative revisions and visible failure**: **partial** — Provider connection state and message ordering sit between the authoritative change store and the browser, requiring explicit revision reconciliation to guarantee no silent gaps.
+
+#### Summary
+
+Fastest path to low-latency push with less raw socket ops, traded for recurring vendor fees, provider coupling, and publication reconciliation.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets** is selected.
+
+It wins on the major criterion—**vendor independence and cost predictability**—while also providing **strong** update freshness and burst efficiency and **strong** alignment with authoritative revisions and visible connectivity failure. Five-second HTTPS polling avoids stateful connection management but accepts multi-second lag and continuous read amplification across 800 browsers. A hosted realtime service reduces raw socket termination ops but introduces recurring vendor fees, provider coupling, and an extra reconciliation boundary between the authoritative store and browsers.
+
+This selection turns on the HLD tenets *A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route* and *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*, keeping revision ordering and disconnect detection directly under MarketBoard's control without a third-party provider in the path.
+
+### Implications
+
+- **Forecloses**: Stateless request/response-only delivery (as in polling) and delegating WebSocket termination to an external realtime vendor.
+- **Obligates `change-store`**: Must persist replayable event revisions for at least the 20-minute recovery window (within the 7-day retention goal) and provide reliable publication to delivery.
+- **Obligates `delivery`**: Must manage WebSocket sessions, authentication/subscription scoping, and connection capacity for at least 800 simultaneous browsers.
+- **Obligates `browser-sync`**: Must implement socket subscription, explicit disconnect visibility, exponential-backoff reconnect, and revision-based catch-up against `change-store`.
