 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed changes are pushed to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Five-second polling offers simpler stateless operation but adds repeated reads and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service reduces connection-management operations but introduces recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets deliver fast updates without vendor lock-in, at the cost of owning connection capacity (800 simultaneous browsers), reliable publication, and reconnect/catch-up logic across the change store, delivery, and browser sync nodes. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery-transport.md`.
